<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Necho II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 610–595 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pharaoh Necho II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Necho II was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Twenty-sixth Dynasty in the late seventh and early sixth centuries BCE. He intervened in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in support of the collapsing Assyrian state. His defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carchemish">Battle of Carchemish</a> in 605 BCE secured the Levant for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/necho-ii.jpg" alt="Necho II" width="172" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keith Schengili-Roberts · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Necho-KnellingStatue_BrooklynMuseum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Necho II, sometimes written Nekau, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psammetichus I</a> and his Great Royal Wife Mehtenweskhet. His royal name, Wahem-Ib-Re, means carrying out the heart, that is the wish, of Re. When he came to the throne the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> had ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Asia</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> shatter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, which was reduced to a remnant at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a> under a general who took the throne name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-uballit-ii">Ashur-uballit II</a>. Necho sent a force to help it on his coronation, but it was too small and the combined armies retreated west across the Euphrates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the spring of 609 BCE Necho led a large army, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, along the coast route into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with his fleet beside it. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a>, according to 2 Kings 23, he engaged the Judean king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a>, who was killed. Necho took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Kadesh</a> on the Orontes and, with Ashur-uballit, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>, becoming the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> to cross it since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>, but failed to take it. He then deposed Josiah's chosen successor, Jehoahaz, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoiakim">Jehoiakim</a> king, taking Jehoahaz to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as a prisoner.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> at Carchemish in 605 BCE and conquered Egyptian territory from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> to the Brook of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Necho could not recover it, and he turned to alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carians">Carians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>.</p><p>He began a canal from the Pelusiac branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, the earliest precursor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a>, but never finished it, and he founded the city Per-Temu Tjeku at Tell el-Maskhuta. He formed an Egyptian navy by recruiting displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> who in three years sailed from the Red Sea round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; scholars differ on whether the account is credible.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Necho died in 595 BCE and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-ii">Psamtik II</a>, who apparently removed Necho's name from almost all his father's monuments for unknown reasons, though Roberto Gozzoli doubts this, calling the evidence fragmentary and contradictory. Egyptologist Donald B. Redford observed that Necho was a man of action from the start, with an imagination beyond that of his contemporaries, who had the misfortune of giving the impression of being a failure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Necho II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Battle of Carchemish in 605 BCE, won by the crown prince Nebuchadnezzar II against the Egyptian army of <b>Pharaoh Necho II</b>, secured the Levant and turned a regional kingdom into the dominant power of the ancient Near East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 609 BCE Josiah was killed at Megiddo confronting the pharaoh <b>Necho II</b>, who then installed Jehoiakim as his vassal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus tells how Phoenician sailors sent by the pharaoh <b>Necho II</b> sailed round Africa in three years.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Necho II</b> began a canal from the Nile to the Red Sea, sent a Phoenician fleet around Africa and killed King Josiah of Judah at Megiddo, but lost to Babylon at Carchemish in 605 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
